\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Field reflection and reflection of observables}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A [spinor time map](fdip-017Z) acts on field values,
and \ref{fdip-0182} compares linear operators at reflected
connections. For a complex function #{f} of a real scalar
configuration #{\phi}, the [observable reflection](fdip-007M)
instead gives #{(\Theta f)(\phi)=\overline{f(\theta_s\phi)}}.
It preserves multiplication order. Its positive-half pairing
integrates #{(\Theta f)f} against the scalar measure under
the hypotheses of \ref{fdip-007O}.}

\p{Menotti and Pelissetto use a site-plane reflection in their
four-dimensional Wilson action. Their reflection exchanges a
Grassmann field with its conjugate field at the reflected
site, inserting their time Dirac matrix #{\gamma_4}. It also
reflects the oriented links, conjugates scalar coefficients
and reverses the order in a product of Grassmann generators
\citet{§2, (2.5)-(2.6) and the following sentence, p. 370}{menottipelissetto1987positivity}.
The time direction in the [spinor time map](fdip-017Z) is
#{t}. Its value action uses the supplied #{\Gamma\varepsilon_t}
and inverse antiunitary.}

\p{Their inequality
\citet{§2, (2.4), p. 370}{menottipelissetto1987positivity}
gives a nonnegative integrated reflected pairing of gauge-invariant
observables supported in their positive-time region. Here the
hopping parameter is nonnegative and less than #{1/6}.
Its domain, integration and order reversal belong to the
observable algebra. The Wilson-action hypotheses are discussed
in \ref{fdip-00JB}.}
